  "url": "sec-representation-of-sets.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "1.2",
  "title": "Representation of Sets",
  "body": " Representation of Sets       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.1 Sets    Specific Learning Outcomes  Represent sets in different situations    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: three ways to describe a set (in words, by listing, in set-builder notation); dots for a pattern; natural numbers, whole numbers and integers.  Do: list a set from its rule, write a rule for a listed set, and list sets given by inequalities.  Apply: class registers and birthdays.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners describe the same set by a list and by a rule.  Teacher's Role Put the table of birth months on the board, or collect the class's own months. Pairs write the set of learners born in June in two ways, then compare.     Born in the Same Month   Ten learners in a group gave the months in which they were born.   Birth Months of Ten Learners   Learner Month Learner Month  Atieno March Faraji June  Baraka June Gathoni August  Chebet June Halima November  Daudi November Imani March  Esther March Jabari October       List , the set of learners in the group born in June.  .    Write as a rule, in the form .  .    List , the learners born in March, and give .  ; .    List the learners in the group born in January.  Nobody: the set is , a set with no elements.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the three ways of describing a set and set-builder notation.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: parts (a) and (b) describe the same set by a list and by a rule.  In set-builder notation, the colon is read such that , and the kind of number is always stated.  Dots show a pattern that goes on, or a long list whose middle is left out.      Three Ways to Describe a Set  A set can be described in three ways.  In words: is the set of odd numbers between and .  By listing (the roster method): .  In set-builder notation : , read the set of all such that is an odd number and is between and .    Dots show a pattern: goes on for ever, while stops at . Some books write a vertical bar instead of the colon: .    Kinds of Numbers    Natural numbers :  Whole numbers :  Integers :    In this book, between and leaves out and . Always say which kind of number a rule uses.   The colon in joins the list of symbols in : it is read such that .   Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       From a Rule to a List   List the elements of .    is left out (the sign is ) and is included (the sign is ). So the set is .     From a List to a Rule   Write each set in set-builder notation: (a) ; (b) .    (a) . (b) . Other correct rules are possible.     A Rule with an Inequality   List the elements of .    Solve the inequality first: , so . The whole numbers less than are , so the set is .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example {2, 5, 9} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Type the empty set as {} . Type a count as a whole number.            List the elements of .     List the elements of .   gives , so . The natural numbers less than are to .    Write in set-builder notation.  For example . Other correct rules are accepted.    List the elements of .     Write in set-builder notation. Does the set have a last element?  . No: the odd numbers go on for ever.    Challenge: Two Rules, One Set  Give two different set-builder descriptions of .  For example and .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Work through the activity, and . Give a card: natural ; whole ; integers . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: describe the set of all learners in your class in three different ways.      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
