  "id": "subsec-using-the-pattern-of-an-expansion",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-using-the-pattern-of-an-expansion.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "2.1.2",
  "title": "Using the Pattern of an Expansion",
  "body": " Using the Pattern of an Expansion  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the pattern of holds whatever and are; the signs alternate for .  Do: expand binomials such as , and by substituting into the pattern, with brackets.  Apply: check an expansion by substituting a number; work out and from .     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find that substituting into the pattern of gives the same result as multiplying out, and see where the signs alternate.  Teacher's Role Work in pairs: in part (a) partner A multiplies and partner B substitutes; they swap roles for part (b). Insist on brackets round each substituted term. Respect: each partner explains their method before the pair compares answers.     Two Ways to the Same Answer   Work in pairs.   Partner A expands by multiplication. Partner B puts and into . Compare your answers.  Both give ; for example .    Both partners expand by substituting and . Which terms change sign compared with (a), and why?  . The terms with an odd power of change sign, because an odd power of is .    Check your answer to (b) by putting on both sides.  and .    Write down the expansion of without multiplying.  .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise substituting into the pattern and the sign rule.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: both partners reached the same answer in (a); the pattern saves the multiplying.  Substitute whole terms with brackets: , not .  Odd powers of a negative number are negative, so the signs alternate for . Check with .      Substituting into the Pattern  To expand , write down the coefficients for power (for power 3: 1, 3, 3, 1; for power 4: 1, 4, 6, 4, 1), then put and into each term, with brackets. For example, in put and : and .  For the terms with an odd power of are negative, so the signs alternate in descending powers of . Write an expansion in descending powers of the first term of the bracket as it is printed: starts with the constant , so its expansion is in ascending powers of .    Check With a Number  Put (or another easy number) into the bracket and into your expansion: the two values must agree. A check cannot prove an expansion right, but a disagreement always shows a mistake.    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Expanding  Expand .   Put and into : Check with : and .     Expanding  Expand .   Put and into the pattern for power 4: The bracket starts with 1, so the expansion is written in ascending powers of . The odd powers of are negative.     Two Letters  Expand .   Put and : Check with : and .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type powers with ^ and fractions with \/ , for example 8x^3-12x^2+3\/2*x-1 .                Expand .   .    Expand in ascending powers of .   .    Expand .   .    Expand .   , and , .    Use to find , and to find , without a calculator.  ;  . .    , where and are positive. Find , , and .  , , ,  The constant term is , so . The term in is , so and . Then and .    Challenge: A Term Without  Expand and write down the term that does not contain .  Put and , and use .  ; the term is .  . The middle term, , does not contain .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Focus on activity parts (a) to (c). Provide a substitution card with the empty pattern . Use and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find the term in in (answer: ; the terms are , , , , ).      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
