  "id": "subsec-approximating-powers-of-numbers",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-approximating-powers-of-numbers.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "2.3.1",
  "title": "Approximating Powers of Numbers",
  "body": " Approximating Powers of Numbers  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a number near 1 (or near a whole number ) can be written (or ) with small; the later terms of the expansion shrink fast.  Do: estimate powers such as , and to 4 decimal places, and decide how many terms are needed.  Apply: compare an estimate with a calculator value.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners estimate and with three and four terms of an expansion and compare with a calculator.  Teacher's Role Work in pairs: one learner works out the terms, the other uses the calculator only at the end of each part. Collect the pairs' sums in a table on the board.     Close to One      Expand .  .    Put and work out each term as a decimal.  , , , , , .    Add the first two, the first three and the first four terms. Compare each sum with a calculator value of .  , , ; the calculator gives .    Repeat for , using in .  The terms are , , , , , ...; the sums are , , ; the calculator gives .    How many terms do you need to give each value correct to 4 decimal places?  Four in both cases: rounds to like the calculator value, but gives ; rounds to , but gives .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise estimating a power from the first few terms of an expansion.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: in the board table each term is much smaller than the one before, so the sums settle quickly.  Choose small: , , .  Stopping rule: keep adding terms until the next term is smaller than , then round to 4 decimal places. Check with a calculator.      Using an Expansion to Find a Power  To estimate a power of a number close to 1 (or close to a whole number ):  write the number as (or ) with small, keeping its sign;  expand (or ) using Pascal's triangle;  work out each term exactly as a decimal, and keep adding terms until the next term is smaller than ;  round the sum to 4 decimal places.    When is small, each term is much smaller than the one before, so a few terms give the value to the accuracy you need.    Spreadsheet Check  In a spreadsheet, put in column A, the numbers from the row of Pascal's triangle in column B, and each term in column C (for with , type =B1*0.02^A1 and fill down). In column D keep a running total, for example =SUM(C$1:C1) . Watch how the total settles after a few rows.    Drag and to compare (solid) with its 2-term (dashed), 3-term (dotted) and 4-term (long dashes) approximations. For large and far from 0 the curves leave the graph; the table still gives the values.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Estimating  Use the expansion of to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   , so put into : The next term is , smaller than , so stop. The sum is , so . A calculator gives , which also rounds to .     Estimating  Use the expansion of to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   , so put into : The fifth term, , is not smaller than , so it is included; the next term is , so stop there. The sum is , so . A calculator gives , also . The terms alternate in sign because is negative.     Estimating  Use the expansion of to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   Put . Each term has a falling power of 2: The sum is , so . A calculator gives .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type decimals with a point, for example 1.1041 . Give answers to the accuracy asked for.                Estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   ; the next term is . So .    Estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   With : ; the next term is . So .    Use the expansion of to find , correct to 4 decimal places. Why is the full sum of the terms exact?  ; has only five terms  , so . The expansion of a fourth power has only five terms, so adding all of them gives the exact value.    Expand up to the term in , and use your answer to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.  ;  . Since , put : , so .    What value of would you use to estimate (a) from (b) from ?  (a) (b)    Estimate using , correct to 2 decimal places.   , so .    Challenge: When Is Not Small  Use the first four terms of to estimate , and compare with a calculator. Why is this estimate poorer than the one for ?  Work out the fifth term.  ; the calculator gives . With the fifth term is , far from negligible.  . The fifth term is , so the later terms still matter: is not small. For the fifth term is only .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Provide a term table with columns , row number, and term. Focus on and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: without a calculator, which is larger, or ? (Answer: is larger, because of the third and later terms.)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
