  "id": "subsec-direct-and-inverse-proportion",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-direct-and-inverse-proportion.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "3.2.1",
  "title": "Direct and Inverse Proportion",
  "body": " Direct and Inverse Proportion  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: direct proportion (the ratio stays the same), inverse or indirect proportion (the product stays the same), and relations that are neither.  Do: test a table for direct or inverse proportion; solve proportion problems by the unitary method.  Apply: buying, sharing work and transport.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners complete three tables and find what stays the same in each.  Teacher's Role For each table ask: What happens to when doubles?     What Stays the Same?   Complete each table, then look for something that stays the same.     Exercise books cost Ksh each. Find the cost of , , , and books.  Ksh , , , and . Cost books is always .    Weeding the school garden takes learner-hours. How long do , , , and learners take?  , , , and hours. Learners hours is always .    Hiring a bus costs Ksh plus Ksh for each kilometre. Find the cost for , and .  Ksh , and . Neither cost km ( , , ) nor cost km stays the same.    In which table does double when doubles? In which does it halve? In which does it do neither?  Doubles in (a); halves in (b); neither in (c): from to the cost goes from to , which is not double.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise direct and inverse proportion and the unitary method.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: table (a) has a fixed ratio (direct), table (b) a fixed product (inverse), and table (c) neither.  Test the ratio and the product. One goes up and the other goes down is not enough for inverse proportion.  The unitary method finds the value for one unit first.      Direct and Inverse Proportion  is directly proportional to when is the same for every pair, so : doubling doubles . The ratio stays the same.  is inversely proportional to when is the same for every pair, so : doubling halves . The product stays the same. Inverse proportion is also called indirect proportion ; in this book the two names mean the same thing.  A relation can be neither : a fixed amount added on (a hire charge plus a rate per kilometre), or a quantity that falls but not inversely (water left in a tank).  The unitary method works out the value for one unit first, then builds up: for direct proportion divide then multiply; for inverse proportion multiply then divide.    Graphs of direct proportion, inverse proportion, and a fixed charge plus a rate.   Direct proportion is a straight line through the origin, inverse proportion is a curve that never meets the axes, and a fixed charge gives a line that starts above the origin.      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Iron Sheets   iron sheets cost Ksh . Find the cost of sheets.    More sheets cost more: direct proportion.  sheets cost Ksh . 1 sheet costs , Ksh . sheets cost , Ksh .     Clearing the Compound   learners clear the school compound in hours. How long would learners take, working at the same rate?    More learners take less time: inverse proportion.  learners take hours. 1 learner would take hours: the job is learner-hours. learners take hours.     Which Kind?   Say whether each table shows direct proportion, inverse proportion or neither.  (a) Buses and trips each bus makes : , , , .  (b) Hours a posho mill runs and kg ground : , , , .  (c) Days and litres left in a tank : , , , .    (a) every time: inverse proportion.  (b) every time: direct proportion.  (c) falls as rises, but the products , , change: neither. Falling is not enough for inverse proportion.     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.            of sugar cost Ksh . Find the cost of .  Ksh  1 kg costs , so cost .    buses each make trips to take learners to a sports day. How many trips would each of buses make?  trips  The job is bus-trips, so each of buses makes .    A store of feed lasts cows for days. How long would it last cows?  days  cow-days, and .    Say whether each table shows direct proportion, inverse proportion or neither.  (a) : ; : .  (b) : ; : .  (c) : ; : .  (a) inverse ( ) (b) direct ( ) (c) neither    A school's water tank lasts learners days. more learners join the school. How long does the tank last now?  days  learner-days, and .    Challenge: Falling Is Not Enough  Explain why goes down when goes up does not prove inverse proportion, and give a table that shows it.  For example : give , with products .  Inverse proportion needs to stay the same. In the table above falls as rises, but the products change, so it is not inverse proportion.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Activity parts (a) and (b), and . Give a card: ratio fixed: direct; product fixed: inverse . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: if is inversely proportional to and is multiplied by , what happens to ? (Answer: it is multiplied by .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
