  "id": "subsec-mixing-to-a-target-price",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-mixing-to-a-target-price.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "3.4.2",
  "title": "Mixing to a Target Price",
  "body": " Mixing to a Target Price  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the balance rule for a target price; profit as a percentage of the cost price.  Do: find the ratio in which to mix two grades for a target price; find how much of one grade to add; work back from a selling price and a percentage profit.  Apply: traders' blends of coffee, tea, rice and beans.     Curriculum Links   Key Inquiry Question Revisit the key inquiry questions ( ) with .  Core Competencies Critical thinking and problem solving (checking that each answer makes sense); communication and collaboration (group work in every activity).  Values Responsibility; respect.  Pertinent and Contemporary Issues Citizenship: equity and non-discrimination, in fair shares by contribution and by work done, safe working hours, and honest mixtures sold at stated prices.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find by trial the mixes of two coffee grades that cost Ksh and Ksh a kilogram, and discover the balance rule.  Teacher's Role Ask: How far is each grade's price from the target?     Hitting the Target Price   A trader sells grade AA coffee at Ksh a kilogram and grade AB at Ksh a kilogram.     Find the cost of 1 kg of AA and AB mixed in the ratios , and .  Ksh , Ksh and Ksh .    Which mix costs Ksh a kilogram, and which Ksh ?  AA : AB costs Ksh ; AA : AB costs Ksh .    For Ksh : each kilogram of AA is Ksh above the target and each kilogram of AB is Ksh below it. How many kilograms of AB balance 1 kg of AA?  , since .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the balance rule, the equation method and profit on cost.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (c) is the balance rule: distances from the target, crossed over.  Always check the ratio by costing the mixture.  Profit is a percentage of the cost price, not of the selling price.      Mixing to a Target Price  To mix a cheap grade and a dear grade so that the mixture costs a target price, balance the distances from the target:   so cheap : dear (dear price target) : (target cheap price). The same rule comes from an equation: the total cost of the mixture equals the target price times its total mass.  When a mixture is sold at a profit of on the cost, selling price cost price , so cost price selling price .    Balancing two grades of coffee on a price line.   On a price line the target 1400 is 100 above AB and 200 below AA, so 2 kg of AB balance 1 kg of AA.      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Tea Grades   A tea packer mixes grade PF1 tea at Ksh a kilogram with grade BP1 at Ksh a kilogram, so that the blend costs Ksh a kilogram. In what ratio are they mixed?    PF1 is below the target and BP1 is above it. Crossing over:  PF1 : BP1 .  Check: .     How Much to Add?   A trader has of beans that cost Ksh a kilogram. How many kilograms of beans at Ksh must be added so that the mixture costs Ksh a kilogram?    Let kg be added. The total cost equals times the total mass:  so , and .  By the balance rule: , which gives the same answer.     Mixing for a Profit   A shopkeeper mixes rice that cost Ksh and Ksh a kilogram and sells the mixture at Ksh a kilogram, making a profit of on the cost. In what ratio is the rice mixed?    The cost price of the mixture is , Ksh a kilogram.  Cheap : dear .     Proportion in Daily Life  Ratios and proportions let a group share a profit fairly by what each member put in; they let a contractor plan workers and days, and a company plan drivers so that no one drives for too long; and they let a trader price a mixture honestly, so that customers pay for what they get. How fast people and machines work decides how many are needed, and how long they can safely work.    Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example 45326 . Give a fraction as a fraction, for example 7\/12 , or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.            In what ratio must coffee at Ksh and Ksh a kilogram be mixed so that the mixture costs Ksh a kilogram?   .    Maize at Ksh and Ksh a kilogram is mixed so that the mixture costs Ksh a kilogram. In what ratio?     of milk at Ksh a litre are mixed with milk at Ksh a litre so that the mixture costs Ksh a litre. How many litres at Ksh are used?   , so .    Tea at Ksh and Ksh a kilogram is mixed and sold at Ksh a kilogram, a profit of on the cost. Find the cost of 1 kg of the mixture and the ratio.  Ksh ;  , and .    of milk and water for chai are in the ratio . How much milk must be added to make the ratio ?   Milk and water . With of water, needs of milk, so add .    Challenge: An Impossible Target  Why can no mixture of maize at Ksh and Ksh a kilogram cost Ksh a kilogram?  The cost of a mixture always lies between the two prices, here between Ksh and Ksh .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) The activity, and . Give a card: cross the distances . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension (digital): build a spreadsheet that costs any mix of two grades and finds the ratio for a target price typed in.      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
