  "id": "subsec-deriving-the-identity",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-deriving-the-identity.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "9.1.1",
  "title": "Deriving the Identity <span class=\"process-math\">\\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\)<\/span>",
  "body": " Deriving the Identity  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: sine and cosine as ratios of sides and as unit-circle coordinates; why their squares add to 1; the difference between an identity and an equation.  Do: derive the identity from any right-angled triangle; check it at any angle; obtain and .  Apply: decide whether a statement is an identity.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners measure and discover that , then explain it with Pythagoras.  Teacher's Role Put learners in groups of four, each learner with one angle ( , , or ), a ruler and a protractor. A hypotenuse of stands for 1 unit. After part (b) ask: Why is your answer not exactly 1? (measurement). Combine the four results of each group on the board, so every group sees all four angles.     Triangles with Hypotenuse 1   Each learner in your group takes one angle : , , or .     Draw a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse and one angle equal to your angle . Measure the side opposite and the side adjacent to , to the nearest .  Opposite and adjacent: and for ; and for ; and for ; and for (all in cm).    Take as 1 unit, so and . Work out .  , , and : each is very close to 1. The small differences come from measuring.    Write , and for the opposite side, the adjacent side and the hypotenuse of any right-angled triangle. Use Pythagoras to show that .  , because .    A right-angled triangle cannot have an angle of . Use the unit circle from Grade 11 ( ) to check the result at .  The point is , and .    Use the interactive in : drag the point all the way round the circle. Does the sum ever change?  No. It stays 1, even when or is negative, because squares are never negative.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the identity the learners measured, prove it, and extend it to every angle.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the four measured sums are close to 1 only because of measurement; the algebra in part (c) shows the sum is exactly 1.  The unit circle in parts (d) and (e) extends the result from acute angles to every angle.  An identity is true for every angle; checking a few angles never proves one, but one angle that fails disproves one.      The Pythagorean Identity  An identity is a statement that is true for every value of for which both sides are defined. For every angle , This is the Pythagorean identity : the three sides of a right-angled triangle obey Pythagoras, so the squares of sine and cosine always add to one.   means , the square of the sine. It is not , which is the sine of . The identity can be rearranged as or .    A right-angled triangle with sides , and , and the same shape with hypotenuse 1.   A right-angled triangle with angle x, opposite side o, adjacent side a and hypotenuse h; beside it the same shape with hypotenuse 1, adjacent side cos x and opposite side sin x.      The point at on the unit circle.   The point at 150 degrees on a circle of radius 1 has coordinates (-0.87, 0.5). Dashed lines run from the point to each axis.      Drag round the circle. The sides of the triangle change, and so do their signs, but stays 1.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Checking the Identity at  Use a calculator to check that when .   and (to 4 decimal places). Both ratios are negative at , but their squares are positive, so the identity still holds.     Deriving the Identity from a Triangle  A right-angled triangle has sides , and . The angle is opposite the side . Show that , then show the same for any right-angled triangle.   and , so It works because . In any right-angled triangle with sides , and hypotenuse , Pythagoras gives , so     Two More Identities  Show that and that whenever . Check the second at .   In the triangle, . Grade 11 found the same result on the unit circle.  Divide every term of by : At : and , so the left side is and the right side is .     Identity or Equation?   is true at , and , but at the left side is . So it is an equation, not an identity. One value of where the two sides differ is enough to show a statement is not an identity. Trying a few values that work proves nothing: to show a statement is an identity, use algebra.    Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Choose from each list.        Verify that for , and . Give each ratio to 4 decimal places.  : , ; : , ; : , . Each sum of squares is to 4 decimal places.    A right-angled triangle has sides , and , and is the angle opposite the side . Write down and , and show that .  , ; .    Use the identity to explain why for every angle .  because . A number whose square is at most 1 lies between and .    Divide by to obtain a third identity. Check it at .  (where ). At : and .  , and . At , .    Show that is not an identity.  At the left side is , not 1. One counterexample is enough.    Challenge: A Point on a Circle  The point lies on a circle of radius 13 with centre . Show that , and find the angle measured from the positive -axis to .  and . Which quadrant is in?  ; .  is in the second quadrant. , and , as it should be.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Provide a triangle template with , and marked. Work through activity parts (a) to (c) and only. Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find and when and is acute, using . (Answer: , so and .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
