  "title": "Using the Identity",
  "body": " Using the Identity  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: one ratio and the quadrant fix the other ratios; the identity changes into and back.  Do: find the other ratios exactly in any quadrant; simplify expressions; prove short identities.  Apply: find the ratios of the pitch of a roof without finding the angle.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find and from without finding , then see that the quadrant decides the sign.  Teacher's Role Put the quadrant signs from Grade 11 on the board. Learners work in pairs. After part (b) ask: Could the roof angle be obtuse? What would change?     The Pitch of a Roof   A fundi building a classroom in Eldoret sets the roof so that its pitch angle has .     Without finding , use the identity to find .  , so (the pitch angle is acute).    Find as a fraction.  .    An obtuse angle has the same sine, . Find its cosine.  : cosine is negative in the second quadrant.    Find both angles between and whose sine is , and say in which quadrant each lies.  (first quadrant) and (second quadrant).        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To set out the steps from one ratio to the others, and how to simplify and prove with the identity.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: parts (a) and (c) gave the same size of cosine with different signs: the quadrant decides.  Square, subtract from 1, take the square root with , choose the sign by the quadrant; then from the two values.  To prove an identity, start from the more complicated side.      From One Ratio to the Others  If you know (or ) and the quadrant of :  square the ratio you know;  subtract from 1 to get the square of the other ratio;  take the square root, remembering ;  choose the sign for the quadrant: sine is positive in the first and second quadrants, cosine in the first and fourth, tangent in the first and third. Then .    To simplify with the identity, look for , or and replace it. To prove an identity, start from the more complicated side and turn it into the other side; never work on both sides at once.    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Cosine Known, Second Quadrant  Given that and , find and .   , so . In the second quadrant sine is positive: .     Sine Known, Third Quadrant  Given that and , find the exact values of and .   , so . In the third quadrant cosine is negative: . Tangent is positive in the third quadrant, as it should be.     Simplifying with the Identity  Simplify .   Replace by : Check at : and (to 3 decimal places).     Proving an Identity  Prove that .   Start with the left side and expand both brackets: The terms cancel, and the identity finishes the proof.     Choose the Sign  gives , but too. The quadrant decides which sign is right. A calculator's inverse key gives only one angle, and so only one sign.    Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type fractions with a slash, for example -12\/13 . Type as sin(x)^2 , for example 3*cos(x)^2 . Type decimals with a point, for example -0.8 .                Given that and , find the exact values of and .  , .    Given that and , find and .  , .    Given that and , find and .  , .  A 3, 4, 5 triangle gives the sizes and ; in the third quadrant sine and cosine are both negative.    Simplify       (a) (b) (c) (d)  (b) . (c) . (d) .    Prove that .  .    Prove that .  .    Write in terms of only.  .    Challenge: Sum Given, Product Wanted  An angle has . Find .  Square both sides.   , so .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Provide a four-step card (square, subtract from 1, square root with , sign from the quadrant). Work through and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: prove that . (Answer: by , ; or write and get .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
