  "number": "9.3.1",
  "title": "Equations That Become Quadratics",
  "body": " Equations That Become Quadratics  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: an equation with and (or and ) becomes a quadratic in one ratio; roots outside to give no angles.  Do: substitute, rearrange, factorise, reject impossible roots, and find all the angles in .  Apply: check solutions on a graph.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners solve and check the solutions on a graph.  Teacher's Role Before part (a) ask: Grade 11 solved . Why can't we factorise this one straight away? Learners work in pairs and share their answers with the next pair before part (e), which uses the interactive.     Two Ratios Become One   Solve for .     Replace using the identity.  .    Rearrange into a quadratic equation in . Write for .  , so .    Factorise and solve for .  , so or .    Find every with .  : and . : and . So .    In the interactive in , set and compare the meeting points with your answers.  Four points, at , , and .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To set out the five steps for solving with the identity.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (a) changed two ratios into one; part (c) was a quadratic the learners already knew how to solve.  Reject any root outside to ; find both angles for each root; check the ends of the interval.      Solving with the Identity  Change every ratio into the same ratio: then it is a quadratic you already know how to solve.  Replace: if the equation has , replace by ; if it has , replace by .  Rearrange into a quadratic in that ratio, with every term on one side.  Solve the quadratic.  Reject any value outside to : and can never be bigger than 1 or smaller than .  Find all the angles in for each value that is left.      (solid) and (dashed).   The curve meets the line y = 1 at 0, 120, 240 and 360 degrees.  The solid curve starts at 1 when x = 0, rises to 2.125 at about 75.5 degrees, falls to -1 at 180 degrees, rises to 2.125 at about 284.5 degrees and returns to 1 at 360 degrees. The dashed line y = 1 meets it at 0, 120, 240 and 360 degrees.      The -values where the curve meets the line solve .     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Replacing  Solve for .   The equation has , so replace by :  gives . : the calculator gives ; sine is negative in the third and fourth quadrants, so or . So .     Four Solutions  Solve for .   Replace by :  gives and ; gives and . So .     A Root to Reject  Solve for .   Replace by :  is impossible, because is never bigger than 1. gives and . So .     Both Ends Count  The interval includes both ends. So gives two solutions, and . But gives only .    Check with a Graph  Graphing software can check your answers: plot both sides of the equation, with the angle unit set to degrees, and read the -values where the graphs cross. Use the apps your school has approved.    Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Type a set of angles in curly brackets, separated by commas, in any order, for example {30, 150, 270} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Give angles that are not whole numbers to 1 decimal place. Type powers with ^ , for example 2c^2-7c+3=0 .            Solve each equation for .        (a) (b) (c) (d) (e)  (a) ; is impossible. (b) . (c) ; is impossible. (d) . (e) .      Show that can be written as .  Factorise .  Solve for .   (b) (c)  (a) , so and . (c) gives and ; gives .    Challenge: Only Two Angles  Solve for , and explain why only two angles appear.  Replace , then factorise.  . The quadratic has a root , which is impossible.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Provide a step frame (replace, rearrange, factorise, reject, angles). Work through . Assign parts (a) and (b) of .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign and .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: use the interactive to find the greatest for which has a solution. (Answer: , where : and .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
