  "id": "subsec-stretching-waves",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-stretching-waves.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "9.4.1",
  "title": "Stretching Waves",
  "body": " Stretching Waves  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: a one-way stretch has an invariant axis and a stretch factor; and are stretches.  Do: describe the stretches that map or onto or ; find images of points; write the equation after stretches.  Apply: change the amplitude and the period of a wave on purpose.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners stretch a traced wave and name the new equation.  Teacher's Role Give out tracing paper and the graph of from . Pairs share one tracing and check each other's points. After part (a) ask: Which points did not move? (those on the -axis, the invariant axis). Link to the one-way stretch of .     Stretching on Tracing Paper      Trace for . Double the height of every point (for example, mark and ) and draw the new wave. What is its equation?  . The points on the -axis stay where they are.    Now divide every -coordinate of the original wave by 3 (for example, moves to ). What is the equation of the new wave?  . The points on the -axis stay where they are.    Predict the period of , and check by tracing.  : every -coordinate of is doubled.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To describe the two stretches of a wave in the language of .     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (a) kept the -axis fixed; part (b) kept the -axis fixed.  A multiplier outside stretches in the -direction by that factor.  A multiplier inside stretches in the -direction by : the inside factor works backwards.      Stretching a Wave  In a one-way stretch , every point moves away from the invariant axis by the same factor, the stretch factor .    is the image of under a stretch with the -axis invariant (parallel to the -axis), stretch factor : . The amplitude is multiplied by .  is the image of under a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor : . The period becomes .      Left: (grey) and . Right: (grey) and . The point and its image are marked.   The point (90 degrees, 1) moves to (90 degrees, 3) in the first panel and to (30 degrees, 1) in the second.  Left panel: the grey wave y = sin x and the black wave y = 3 sin x, both crossing the x-axis at 0, 180 and 360 degrees; the black wave reaches 3. Right panel: the grey wave y = sin x and the black wave y = sin 3x, which repeats every 120 degrees and has its first maximum at (30 degrees, 1).      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       A Stretch Parallel to the -Axis  Describe the transformation that maps onto , and find the image of .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 4. The point maps to . The amplitude becomes 4; the period stays .    A Stretch Parallel to the -Axis  Describe the transformation that maps onto , and find the image of .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor . The point maps to . The period becomes .    Two Stretches  Describe the two stretches that map onto , and find the image of .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2, and a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2 (since ). . The amplitude is 2 and the period .    A Negative Multiplier  Describe how the graph of is obtained from .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2, followed by a reflection in the -axis (the minus sign). The amplitude is 2.    Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type decimals with a point, for example 1.73 , or a fraction such as 1\/3 .        Describe the stretch that maps or onto       (a) -axis invariant, stretch factor 5 (b) -axis invariant, stretch factor (c) -axis invariant, stretch factor 3 (d) -axis invariant, stretch factor 0.5    The graph of is stretched with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2. Write down the equation of the image and its period.  ; period .    The graph of is mapped onto . Find the image of the point .     The graph of is mapped onto . Describe both stretches, and find the first maximum point of .  Stretch factor 3 with the -axis invariant; stretch factor with the -axis invariant. First maximum .    A wave with amplitude 2 and period is stretched with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 1.5, then with the -axis invariant, stretch factor . Find the new amplitude and period.  Amplitude 3, period .    Challenge: Does the Order Matter?  Show that the two stretches of give the same graph whichever is done first.  Follow a general point through each order.  Each stretch changes only one coordinate: either order sends to .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Plot points on tracing paper only. Work through and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: which single stretch maps onto ? (Answer: a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
