  "title": "Column and Position Vectors in Three Dimensions",
  "body": " Column and Position Vectors in Three Dimensions       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.6 Vectors II    Specific Learning Outcomes  Express column and position vectors in terms of unit vectors in different situations    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the position vector of is ; ; equal vectors have equal components.  Do: write position vectors, find , find a point from a vector, and find the fourth vertex of a parallelogram.  Apply: describe the corners of a box and the flight of a drone with vectors.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners find that the moves from to equal the position vector of minus the position vector of .  Teacher's Role If possible, stand a real carton with one corner on a marked origin on a desk. After part (b), ask: Did you get the same column by counting moves and by subtracting?     Corners of a Box   A carton long, wide and high has one corner at the origin . It is lettered like the cuboid in : base with on the -axis and on the -axis, and , , , above , , , . Distances are in centimetres.   The carton with the arrows , and .   A box 40 cm along x, 30 cm along y and 20 cm high with a corner at O. Solid arrows go from O to A and from O to F; a dashed arrow goes from A to F.        The position vector of a point is the column of moves from to the point. Write the position vectors of , and .  , , .    Find by counting the moves along the edges from to . Then work out . What do you notice?  Counting: along , along , up, so . Subtracting gives the same column.    Write . How is it related to ?  : the same moves in reverse.    Compare with .  Both are . They are equal vectors: parallel edges of the same length, pointing the same way.    Find .  .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise position vectors and the rule for the vector between two points.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (b) showed that counting moves and subtracting position vectors give the same column.  A position vector always starts at .  : end minus start.  Equal vectors have equal columns, wherever they are drawn.      Position Vectors and the Vector  The position vector of a point is , the journey from the origin to the point. We write it with the small bold letter, . The point has position vector .  A vector such as that is not tied to is a free vector . Going from to is the same as going from back to and then from to , so (end minus start). Going the other way, .  Two vectors are equal when all their components are equal: they have the same length and the same direction, wherever they are drawn.    The vector from to is .   From O, an arrow a goes to A and an arrow b goes to B. A dashed arrow from A to B is labelled AB = b minus a.      Writing Vectors by Hand  Printed books show vectors in bold, . You cannot write bold by hand, so underline the letter ( ) or draw an arrow over the two letters, .    End Minus Start   is the vector from to , not from to . For , start with the end point: .    Click a corner of the box to see its position vector. Turn the box with the view sliders.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Position Vectors of Points   (a) Write the position vector of . (b) The point has position vector . Write the coordinates of and say which coordinate plane it lies in.    (a) : the coordinates written as a column.  (b) . Its -coordinate is , so lies in the -plane.     The Vector Between Two Points   is and is . Find and .    End minus start: and .     Finding a Point from a Vector   (a) is and . Find .  (b) is a parallelogram with , and . Find .    (a) , so is .  (b) In the parallelogram , the sides and are opposite, so . Now , so and is .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type one number in each box of a column vector. Fractions may be typed with a slash, for example 5\/2 .            Write the position vectors of and .  ,    is and is . Find and .  ,    For the carton in , find and .  ,  is , so . is and is , so .    A drone spraying a shamba near Nakuru flies in a straight line from to , in metres. Write as a column.     and is . Find .   .    Challenge: Where the Diagonals Meet  is a parallelogram with , and . Find . Then show that the diagonals and have the same midpoint.  .  ; both midpoints are .  , so . The midpoint of is , and the midpoint of is : the same point.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give a column template with an end row and a start row to subtract. Work through and . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity plus all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: with , , as in (b), find the other two points that make a parallelogram with , and . (Answer: and .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
