  "title": "Parallel Vectors",
  "body": " Parallel Vectors       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.6 Vectors II    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine parallelism of vectors in different situations    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know:  and are parallel when ; the sign of gives the direction; .  Do: test pairs of vectors for parallelism; find and unknown components; find a parallel vector of a given magnitude.  Apply: decide whether pipes or edges are parallel.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners discover the scalar-multiple test for parallel vectors, including vectors in opposite directions.  Teacher's Role Work in pairs. After part (c), ask: Is it enough that two of the components are in the same ratio?     Scalar Multiples   For each pair, decide whether the second vector is a number times the first. If it is, find the number.     and  The second is times the first: parallel, same direction.    and  The second is times the first: parallel, opposite direction.    and  The ratios are , and : not all the same, so not parallel.    and  The second is times the first (and ): parallel, opposite direction. A zero component matches a zero component.    Write a rule for deciding whether two vectors are parallel.  Every component of one is the same multiple of the matching component of the other. If is negative, they point in opposite directions.    In (a), compare the magnitudes of the two vectors.  and : the second is times as long.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the definition of parallel vectors and the test for them.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (c) shows that two matching ratios are not enough: the same must work for every component.  The sign of gives the direction: positive, same; negative, opposite.  Zero components must match, as in part (d).      Parallel Vectors  Two non-zero vectors and are parallel when one is a number times the other, , with the same number for every component. If they point in the same direction; if they point in opposite directions. Their magnitudes are related by .  To test two vectors, divide each component of by the matching component of . If every ratio is the same number (and any zero component of one matches a zero component of the other), the vectors are parallel.    The vector and two vectors parallel to it.   On a square grid: vector a, 2 across and 1 up; vector 2a, twice as long in the same direction; and a dashed vector minus one half a, half as long and pointing the opposite way.      Drag the tip of and move the slider. The vector stays parallel to and turns round when is negative.     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Testing for Parallel Vectors   , and . Is parallel to ? Is parallel to ?    Divide the components of by those of : , , . So : parallel, in opposite directions.  For and : , , . The third ratio is different, so is not parallel to .     Unknown Components   is parallel to . Find and .    Write . The middle components are both known: , so . Then , and gives .     Parallel with a Given Magnitude   . Find the vectors parallel to with magnitude .    . A vector has magnitude , so . The two vectors are (same direction) and (opposite direction).     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type one number in each box of a column vector. Fractions may be typed with a slash, for example 5\/2 . Type a square root as sqrt(41) , or give the decimal to 2 decimal places.                Which of these vectors are parallel to ? Give for each one that is. (a) (b) (c) (d)  (a) yes, (b) yes, , opposite direction (c) no (d) yes,    Sort these six vectors into three parallel pairs: , , , , , .  and ( ); and ( ); and ( )    Find if . What is the ratio of their magnitudes?  ; the first is times as long as the second.    Two straight pipes on a building site in Mombasa run in the directions and . Are the pipes parallel? Do the two direction vectors point the same way?  Parallel: the second is times the first, so the direction vectors point in opposite directions.    is parallel to . Find and .  ,  The third components are both known: , so . Then and , so .    Challenge: Parallel Combinations    Show that and are parallel for any vectors and , and say whether they point the same way.  and . Find so that is parallel to .    Write as a column.  (a) : parallel, opposite directions (b)  (a) , a scalar multiple, so the two are parallel. The scalar is negative, so they point in opposite directions.  (b) . For this to be : and (the same equation), and , so and . Then .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give a ratio table: each component of divided by the matching component of . Focus on activity parts (a) to (d). Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity plus all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find a vector parallel to with magnitude . (Answer: .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
