  "title": "Ratio Theorem",
  "body": " Ratio Theorem       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.6 Vectors II    Specific Learning Outcomes  Derive and use ratio theorem in vector operations    Grade Level  12    Estimated Duration  40 minutes         Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: the ratio theorem for internal and external division, and that the two coefficients add to .  Do: derive the theorem from ; use it with letters and with coordinates; read the ratio from a position vector.  Apply: place a valve on a water pipe; find the centroid of a triangle.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners derive from .  Teacher's Role Each learner draws a triangle on paper, marks and , and divides in the ratio with a ruler. Before part (f), ask: What do the two numbers in front of and add to?     Deriving the Ratio Theorem   In triangle , and . The point on divides it internally in the ratio .   Triangle with on and .   Triangle OAB. An arrow labelled a goes from O to A and an arrow labelled b from O to B. P is on AB, with AP marked m and PB marked n. An arrow goes from O to P.        What fraction of is ?  .    Write in terms of and .  (end minus start).    Use to write in terms of and . Collect the terms.  .    Repeat (a) to (c) for a ratio .  .    Check your formula with , and the ratio . Compare with , part (e).  , the same point .    What do the coefficients of and add to?  , for every ratio.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To formalise the ratio theorem, internal and external, and the meaning of coefficients that add to .     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: the theorem was derived, not memorised; learners can rebuild it from part (d) at any time.  The weights cross over: the weight on is the part of the ratio nearer .  The external version comes from replacing by .      The Ratio Theorem  Let and have position vectors and . The point dividing internally in the ratio has position vector The point dividing externally in the ratio has position vector This is the ratio theorem . The weight on is the part of the ratio nearer . For the midpoint, gives , as in Grade 10.  Both forms come from , so the coefficients always add to . Turning this round: a point with position vector , where , lies on the line . It is between and when , beyond when , and beyond when .    Drag and , and move the and sliders. The two arrows from , a multiple of then a multiple of , always end at the point on .     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Using the Ratio Theorem   The points and have position vectors and . Find the point that divides internally in the ratio .    With and : so is .     External Division by the Ratio Theorem   is and is . Find the point that divides externally in the ratio .     so is . Check: and , so is three times and is beyond .     Finding the Ratio   For each point, decide whether it divides internally or externally, and find the ratio.          (a) The coefficients add to and is between and , so is between and . The weight on is and the weight on is : internally. (Check: .)  (b) , so is on the line , and puts it beyond . and . The lengths are and , so divides externally in the ratio .     Vectors in use: in 3D animation a point is often placed three fifths of the way between two others; the program computes it with exactly the weights of the ratio theorem.   Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type answers in terms of a and b like 3\/5*a+2\/5*b or (3a+2b)\/5 . Type fractions with a slash, for example 3\/4 , not as decimals.                Use the ratio theorem to find the point dividing internally in the ratio , where is and is .     Use the ratio theorem to find the point dividing externally in the ratio , where is and is .   .    In terms of and , write the position vector of the point dividing (a) internally in the ratio ; (b) externally in the ratio .  (a) (b)  (b) . The coefficients and add to , and : the point is beyond .    A water pipe at a school runs straight from a tank to a tap , in metres. A valve divides internally in the ratio . Find the position of .   .    Each point lies on the line . Say whether it divides internally or externally, and give the ratio. (a) (b)  (a) Internally, . (b) Externally, .  (b) and ; is beyond and .    Challenge: The Centroid  In triangle , is the midpoint of . Show that the point dividing internally in the ratio has position vector . Explain why the three medians of the triangle meet at .  The answer is symmetric in , and .  with .  By the ratio theorem, . The same working for the median from (to the midpoint of ) or from gives the same expression, because it does not change when , and are swapped. So the point two thirds of the way along each median is the same point , and all three medians pass through it.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give the derivation as a fill-in sheet with the same three blanks as the first STACK question, then work . Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: show that if with , then . (Answer: .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
