  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "13.1.3",
  "title": "The Second Derivative Test",
  "body": " The Second Derivative Test  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know:  measures how the gradient changes; its sign decides between a maximum and a minimum point; zero decides nothing.  Do: classify stationary points by , falling back on the gradient table.  Apply: find unknown coefficients from a stationary point.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners see that at a maximum point the gradient is decreasing, so its own derivative is negative, and that a zero second derivative settles nothing.  Teacher's Role Return to the Ngong Hills path. After part (c), ask: What if is ? Let groups test and , and hear each group's answer before giving one.     The Gradient of the Gradient   Use the Ngong Hills path from .     From , find , the derivative of the gradient.  .    Find its value at and at .  and .    At the maximum point the gradient goes from positive to negative. Is the gradient increasing or decreasing there? What about at the minimum point?  Decreasing at the maximum point, so ; increasing at the minimum point, so .    For and , find at , and find the nature of the stationary point at the origin from a gradient table.  Both are . has a minimum point ( ); has a point of inflection ( ). A zero second derivative tells you nothing.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To state the second derivative test and its blind spot.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (c) is the reason for the test; part (d) is its blind spot.  When , the test is silent, not a verdict: go back to the gradient table.      The Second Derivative Test  The second derivative , written or , is the derivative of . It is the rate at which the gradient itself is changing. (In Grade 11 you met as acceleration.)  At a stationary point:  if , the point is a minimum point;  if , the point is a maximum point;  if , the test is silent, not a verdict: use the gradient table.      When the Second Derivative Is Zero   has at , but the origin is a minimum point. also has at , and there the origin is a point of inflection. So does not mean a point of inflection. Use the gradient table ( ).    Two curves with at the origin.   Both curves have second derivative 0 at the origin. y = x to the 4 has a minimum point there; y = x cubed has a point of inflection.      Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Using the Second Derivative  Find the stationary points of and use the second derivative to determine their nature.   , so the stationary points are at and : and .  . , so is a maximum point. , so is a minimum point.     When the Test Is Silent  Find the stationary points of and determine their nature.   , zero at and . The points are and .  . At it is , so is a minimum point.  At it is , so the test is silent. Use the gradient either side: at , ; at , . The pattern is , so is a point of inflection.     Finding Coefficients  The curve has a stationary point at . Find and , and determine the nature of the point.   . The gradient is at : . The point lies on the curve: .  Subtracting, , so and . The curve is .  , which is at : is a minimum point.     Differentiation in use: a dairy cooperative that knows when its milk supply peaks and when it dips, as in , can plan its cooling tanks and transport. Name one quantity in your community that rises and falls during the year. When are its stationary points?   Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type whole numbers or fractions, for example -3 or 5\/2 . Type powers with ^ , for example 2x^3-5x+1 . For the nature of a point, choose from the list.                For , find and .  ;    Find the stationary points of and use the second derivative to determine their nature.  maximum point ( ); minimum point ( )    Find the stationary points of and use the second derivative to determine their nature.  minimum point ( ); maximum point ( )    Find the stationary points of and determine their nature.  minimum point; point of inflection  , zero at and . is at (minimum point) and at , so test the gradient: at and at . Both positive, so is a point of inflection.    The curve has stationary points at and . Find and , and determine the nature of each stationary point.  , ; maximum point, minimum point  must be , so and . is at and at .    The milk collected each day at a cooling centre in Ol Kalou, hundred litres, months after the start of January, is modelled by for . Find and classify the stationary points, and say what they mean.  maximum point; minimum point. The most milk, about a day, comes at the start of March; the least, about a day, at the start of June.  . and . is at and at .    Challenge: Every Value of k  Show that for every , the curve has a maximum point at and a minimum point at .  .  is at ; at and at .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Provide a two-column card: minimum point; maximum point; use the gradient table . Work through the first two examples. Assign to .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find a curve with at a stationary point that is a maximum point. (Answer: , or any .)      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
