  "id": "subsec-reversing-differentiation",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "subsec-reversing-differentiation.html",
  "type": "Subsection",
  "number": "14.1.1",
  "title": "Reversing Differentiation",
  "body": " Reversing Differentiation  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: integration reverses differentiation; a function has many integrals, which differ by a constant.  Do: integrate constants and single powers of , and check the answer by differentiating.  Apply: recognise which functions are integrals of a given function.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners discover integration by working backwards from derivatives, and see why a constant appears.  Teacher's Role Do not name integration until after part (c). After part (b), ask: Is there only one answer?     Working Backwards      Differentiate each function: , , , , , , .  , , , , , , .    Which functions have derivative ? Can you find any more?  , and ; in fact plus any constant.    Find a function whose derivative is ; ; ; .  , , , , each plus any constant.    Spot the rule: which function has derivative ?  , because the derivative of is ; dividing by leaves .    Water flows into a tank at a school in Kitui. After minutes the volume litres is increasing at litres per minute. Give a formula for . Why could also be right?  fits. has the same rate of change: the tank may already have held at the start.  Ask learners what extra fact would tell them which formula is right (the volume at ). This leads into .        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To name integration as the reverse of differentiation and state the rule for powers of .     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (b) shows that a derivative does not fix the function: we write .  The rule in words: raise the power by , then divide by the new power.  Every integral can be checked by differentiating it.      Integration   Integration undoes differentiation: if , then . This is the indefinite integral of . The function being integrated, , is the integrand , and is the constant of integration . Differentiating a constant gives , so we cannot tell which constant was there: write .  For and any number , . Check every answer by differentiating it: you should get the integrand back.    Other Powers  The rule works for any power of except , where it would divide by zero. This chapter needs only the powers to .    Drag : the curve moves up and down, but its gradient at every stays the same. That is why an integral needs .     Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       Integrating a Single Power  Find .   Raise the power by to and divide by the new power: Check: .     Constants and Linear Terms  Find (a) and (b) .   (a) , so . Check: the derivative of is .  (b) , so . Check: the derivative of is .     Which Are Integrals?  Which of these functions are integrals of ? , , , .   Differentiate each: , , and . So and are integrals of ; the other two are not. Both integrals have the form .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type powers with ^ and the constant of integration as c , for example x^3\/3+2x+c .            Find (a) (b) (c) (d) .  (a) (b) (c) (d)    Find and check your answer by differentiating.   . Check: .    Copy and complete the table, then write the rule for .   Integrals of Powers of   Function  Integral     , , , (each );    The derivative of a function is . Write down two different functions with this derivative.  For example and : any .    Water flows into a tank at litres per minute. Write down if the tank was empty at , and if it held at .  ;  . At , , so for an empty tank and otherwise.    Challenge: Why a Constant?  The functions and both have derivative . Explain why is a constant.  Differentiate .  The derivative of is , so its graph is horizontal everywhere: is a constant.  . A function whose gradient is at every point has a horizontal graph, so it is a constant. So any two integrals of differ by a constant, which is why one covers them all.     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Focus on activity parts (a) to (c) and the first two examples. Give a differentiate to check card: write the answer, differentiate it, compare with the integrand. Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: find all functions with and (answer: only).      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
