  "number": "14.1.2",
  "title": "Finding the Constant of Integration",
  "body": " Finding the Constant of Integration  Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:  Know: one known point picks out one curve from the family .  Do: integrate, substitute a known pair of values and solve for .  Apply: find an amount from its rate of change and its starting value.     Problem-Solving and Discovery   Suggested Time 14 minutes  Objective Learners see that the same growth rate gives a family of height formulas, and that one measurement fixes the constant.  Teacher's Role Show and ask: What do the three seedlings have in common?     Seedlings at a Tree Nursery   At a tree nursery in Kakamega, seedlings of one kind grow at centimetres per week, where cm is the height weeks after planting.     Integrate to find in terms of .  .    Three seedlings were , and tall when planted. Find the value of for each.  At , , so , and .    Find the height of each seedling after weeks.  , so , and .    A fourth seedling is tall after weeks. Find its value of .  , so .    Why can the growth rate alone not tell you a seedling's height?  Every curve in the family has the same rate of growth; we need one height at a known time to choose the curve.      Heights of seedlings planted at , and .   Three curves of seedling height against weeks, one above another, starting at 3, 5 and 8 cm at week 0 and reaching 43, 45 and 48 cm at week 4.  Horizontal axis time t in weeks from 0 to 4; vertical axis height h in centimetres from 0 to 50. Three curves h = 2t squared + 2t + c: solid for c = 3 (from 3 to 43 cm), dashed for c = 5 (from 5 to 45 cm), dotted for c = 8 (from 8 to 48 cm). Each curve is the one below moved straight up.        Structured Instruction    Suggested Time 8 minutes  Objective To set out the steps for finding the constant of integration.     Key Takeaways & Teacher Connection   Connecting to Learner Work: part (d): one measured height fixes .  Integrate, keep , substitute the known pair, solve for , then write the formula again with the value of .      Finding  To find the equation of a curve from its gradient function and one point on it:  Integrate the gradient function, including .  Substitute the coordinates of the known point.  Solve for .  Write the equation with that value of .  The equation of the curve has no left in it.    Practice and Application     Suggested Time  10 minutes    Objective  To apply the learned concepts and procedures to solve varied problems.    Teacher's Role  The teacher works through the following examples with the class, asking questions and connecting to insights from the learner experience.       A Curve Through a Point  A curve has gradient function and passes through . Find its equation.   Integrate: . At : , so . The curve is .     Using Function Notation  Given and , find .   . Then , so and .     Water in a Tank  Water flows into a tank at a school in Kitui at litres per minute. At the tank holds . Find in terms of , and the volume after minutes.   . At , , so .  After minutes, .     Assessment \/ Evaluate     Suggested Time  8 minutes    Objective  To formatively assess individual Learner understanding of the lesson objectives.    Learning Experience  Learners independently complete a short assessment task (select from the exercises below).    Teacher's Role  Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.       Checkpoint Guide   Teacher's Role      Pair Learners strategically so stronger learners can explain reasoning to peers.    Collect and review the exit tickets to gauge Learner understanding and identify any common misconceptions that need to be addressed in the next lesson.      Instructions to Give Leaners      Click Show new example question to load the problem    Solve the displayed question    Click Submit to check your answer    If incorrect, carefully read the feedback and analyse the error before trying a new question. The immediate feedback from checkpoint submissions allows Learners to identify and correct errors in real-time.    Complete at least 5 questions before rotating      Type powers with ^ and the constant of integration as c , for example x^3\/3+2x+c . In a formula with time, use t , for example 3t^2+2t+5 .            A curve has and passes through . Find its equation.   ; , so .    Given and , find .   ; , so .    A curve has and passes through . Find its equation.   ; at , , so .    A poultry farmer near Thika is growing her flock. After weeks the number of birds is increasing at birds per week. At she has birds. Find in terms of , and the number of birds after weeks.  ; birds  and . Then .    A curve has , where is a constant. It passes through and . Find and the equation of the curve.  ;  . At : . At : , so .    Challenge: Two Points, Two Unknowns  A curve has , where is a constant. It passes through and . Find .  First use to find the constant of integration.   . At : . At : , so .     Differentiation   Differentiation Strategies   Learner Group Strategy and Learning Experience  Struggling Learners (Support) Give a step card: integrate, , substitute, solve, rewrite. Work through the first two examples together. Assign and .  On-Level Learners (Core) Full activity and all examples. Assign to .  Advanced Learners (Challenge) All exercises including . Extension: explain why one point can never fix two unknown constants (one equation cannot fix two unknowns).      Assessment   Assessment methods    Type  Method  Purpose    Formative (During Lesson)   Observation: Teacher circulates during the anchor task, listening to group discussions.  Questioning: Teacher asks probing questions to check for understanding.  Exit Ticket: A short, independent task at the end of the lesson.    To monitor learner progress in real-time, provide immediate feedback, and adjust instruction as needed.     Summative (After Lesson)   Homework assignment with more complex problems.  Future quiz\/test questions.    To evaluate mastery of the learning objectives after instruction and practice.        Teacher Reflection  Complete this after the lesson   What went well?    What would I change?    Learner Understanding: What did the exit tickets reveal about Learner learning?    Next Steps: Based on the assessment data, what is the plan for the next lesson?      "
